// @emit-error unsupported map value type
// A STRUCT that is a MEMBER of any declared union cannot be a map VALUE — a pre-existing
// LOUD reject under BOTH key reps, pinned here with its actual condition because #1286
// filed a different one.
//
// THE FILING SAID: "a program holding BOTH a struct-valued and a union-valued map
// rejects `unsupported map value type` — and the `{[string]: …}` spelling of that same
// pair rejects identically". The pair is not the condition and the union-valued MAP is
// not part of it. Measured, six cells per key rep, master and this branch identical:
//
//     struct-valued map ALONE                                        runs
//     union-valued map ALONE                                         runs
//     struct-valued map + a DISJOINT union-valued map (`{[K]: T|U}`)  RUNS
//     struct-valued map + an OVERLAPPING union-valued map (`S|T`)    rejects
//     struct-valued map + a union ALIAS `S | T`, NO union-valued map rejects
//     the same overlap through a LIST of maps                        rejects
//
// The two that reject are the two where `S` is a MEMBER of a declared union, and the
// fifth line is the one that kills the filed framing: there is no union-valued map in
// this file at all.
//
// THE MECHANISM is `mvValKindOfName`'s last arm, `structIndexByValName(valName) >= 0`.
// Declaring `S | T` moves `S` into the VARIANT table; it is no longer a plain struct, so
// the arm misses and the value kind falls through to the `-3` unsupported sentinel that
// `emitMapNew` reports.
//
// THE SECOND HALF OF THAT PARAGRAPH IS RETIRED, and this note is what remains of it. It
// used to read: "the mv slot's own `mvValStructIdx` column holds a STRUCT index and its
// ref-list intern reads `sNames[vsi]`, which a variant has no entry in. Opening it is a
// variant column on that parallel array". That column now exists — `mvValVariantIdx`,
// pushed in lockstep beside its struct twin — and an arm-valued map DOES lower
// (silent-class-inventory D34, `tests/cases/maps/member-struct-valued-map.vl`). What keeps
// THIS file rejecting is the arm above it: the kind ladder is NAME-keyed and asks first, and
// D34's nominal channel (the annotation node's own value type) is only consulted after the
// kind is decided. Hoist the annotation into an alias — `type M = {[string]: S}` and
// `const a: M = Map()`, nothing else changed — and the identical program runs.
//
// So the reject is SPELLING-dependent rather than value-type-dependent, which is a strictly
// different and worse claim than the one this header used to make; it is filed as
// silent-class-inventory D39 with that one-line control. It is still a clean reject on both
// reps, so it stays in the acceptable class and stays pinned at the CONDITION rather than
// fixed here.
type S = { x: i32 }
type T = { y: i32 }
type Q = S | T
function go() {
  const a: {[string]: S} = Map()
  a["k"] = { x: 1 }
  const q: Q = { y: 3 }
  if q is T { print(q.y) }
  print(a.length)
}
go()
